The fontDict is not stored in the GfxFont class - it's only used during construction. So the fontDict is owned by whoever passed it. Let me trace back who owns the fontDict.

In GfxFontDict constructor:
```cpp
fonts[i] = GfxFont::makeFont(xref, fontDict->getKey(i), r, obj2.getDict());
```

The `fontDict` parameter is the Dict* passed to GfxFontDict constructor. In GfxResources constructor:
```cpp
Dict *resDict = resDictA->copy(xref);
...
const Object &obj1 = resDict->lookupNF("Font");
if (obj1.isRef()) {
    Object obj2 = obj1.fetch(xref);
    if (obj2.isDict()) {
        r = obj1.getRef();
        fonts = new GfxFontDict(xref, &r, obj2.getDict());
    }
} else if (obj1.isDict()) {
    fonts = new GfxFontDict(xref, nullptr, obj1.getDict());
}
...
delete resDict;
```

When `obj1.isRef()`, `obj2` is a local Object that holds the fetched dict. `obj2.getDict()` returns the Dict pointer. When `obj2` goes out of scope (at the end of the if block), its destructor calls `free()` which decrements the dict's ref count. But the GfxFontDict constructor may have stored references to this dict...

Wait, actually the GfxFontDict constructor doesn't store the fontDict either - it just reads from it during construction. Let me verify:

